1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,462
You just turned on a light.

2
00:00:02,462 --> 00:00:05,115
It cost you about two-tenths of a second of work.

3
00:00:05,615 --> 00:00:09,657
In 1800, the same amount of light cost five and a half hours.

4
00:00:10,157 --> 00:00:12,953
In ancient Babylon, more than a full working week.

5
00:00:13,553 --> 00:00:17,513
This is one of the biggest price collapses in human history.

6
00:00:17,513 --> 00:00:22,199
And for almost 200 years, the people whose job was to measure prices...

7
00:00:22,199 --> 00:00:23,321
mostly missed it.

8
00:00:23,421 --> 00:00:25,659
By the end of this video, you'll see why.

9
00:00:25,659 --> 00:00:30,953
And why your great-great-grandparents may have woken up in the middle of every night, on purpose.

10
00:00:32,453 --> 00:00:36,093
Money is a terrible way to compare prices across centuries.

11
00:00:36,093 --> 00:00:42,447
So on this channel, we use something simpler: how long would an ordinary worker have to work to buy it?

12
00:00:43,097 --> 00:00:45,718
Our unit tonight: a thousand lumen-hours.

13
00:00:45,718 --> 00:00:50,704
That's roughly one ordinary household bulb, burning for an hour and a quarter.

14
00:00:51,154 --> 00:00:54,792
To match that one bulb with candles, you'd need about 60 of them.

15
00:00:58,292 --> 00:01:01,047
For most of human history, light meant fire.

16
00:01:01,047 --> 00:01:02,299
And fire meant work.

17
00:01:02,749 --> 00:01:13,330
The economist William Nordhaus estimated that for early humans, a thousand lumen-hours from an open fire cost around 58 hours, mostly spent gathering wood.

18
00:01:13,780 --> 00:01:17,496
Stone lamps burning animal fat brought it down to about 50.

19
00:01:17,946 --> 00:01:23,758
By 1750 BC, Babylon had a market for lamp oil made from sesame.

20
00:01:23,758 --> 00:01:26,895
Better, but still about 41½ hours.

21
00:01:27,345 --> 00:01:28,323
Think about that.

22
00:01:28,323 --> 00:01:36,028
For the light of one modern bulb, burning for about an hour, a Babylonian worker would have worked longer than you do in a whole week.

23
00:01:36,178 --> 00:01:36,908
So mostly...

24
00:01:36,908 --> 00:01:38,123
people went without.

25
00:01:38,123 --> 00:01:40,616
When the sun went down, the day was over.

26
00:01:40,816 --> 00:01:42,870
And then, for thousands of years...

27
00:01:42,870 --> 00:01:44,220
almost nothing changed.

28
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:51,761
By the Middle Ages, candle-making was a real trade.

29
00:01:51,761 --> 00:01:56,308
In 1292, the tax rolls of Paris listed 72 candle makers.

30
00:01:56,758 --> 00:01:59,484
Most candles were tallow, rendered animal fat.

31
00:01:59,484 --> 00:02:02,861
They smoked, they dripped, and they were still expensive.

32
00:02:03,011 --> 00:02:09,585
In 1800, our thousand lumen-hours of tallow candlelight cost about five and a half hours of work.

33
00:02:10,035 --> 00:02:11,110
Better than Babylon.

34
00:02:11,110 --> 00:02:14,551
But imagine working your whole morning for one evening of light.

35
00:02:14,701 --> 00:02:22,257
In the 1700s and 1800s, lamps burning whale oil, and candles made from whales, became the light of choice.

36
00:02:22,507 --> 00:02:26,675
Then, in 1855, a Yale chemist named Benjamin Silliman Jr.

37
00:02:26,675 --> 00:02:28,502
ran a careful experiment.

38
00:02:28,502 --> 00:02:35,229
He burned different fuels side by side, and measured how much light each gave for the money.

39
00:02:35,679 --> 00:02:38,162
One of the fuels on his bench was rock oil.

40
00:02:38,162 --> 00:02:39,605
What we'd call petroleum.

41
00:02:39,755 --> 00:02:43,576
Within fifteen years, kerosene lamps changed everything.

42
00:02:43,576 --> 00:02:48,420
By 1870, the price of our light had fallen to about 20 minutes of work.

43
00:02:48,670 --> 00:02:55,915
As one writer put it in 1938, petroleum gave kerosene to the world, and life to the few remaining whales.

44
00:03:00,465 --> 00:03:06,701
In 1879, Joseph Swan and Thomas Edison each produced a working carbon-filament bulb.

45
00:03:06,701 --> 00:03:09,076
And at first, it was no bargain.

46
00:03:09,076 --> 00:03:12,862
Edison priced his electric light to match gaslight.

47
00:03:13,512 --> 00:03:17,045
Gas had already been lighting city streets for decades.

48
00:03:17,045 --> 00:03:20,192
Pall Mall in London got gas street lamps in 1820.

49
00:03:20,842 --> 00:03:23,686
And when the bulb arrived, gas fought back.

50
00:03:23,686 --> 00:03:29,374
A new invention, the Welsbach mantle, made gas lamps about seven times more efficient.

51
00:03:29,374 --> 00:03:31,491
For a while, it was a real race.

52
00:03:32,141 --> 00:03:37,087
In 1900, our thousand lumen-hours still cost about 13 minutes of work.

53
00:03:37,537 --> 00:03:43,121
But electric light kept getting better, and cheaper, almost every decade.

54
00:03:43,121 --> 00:03:44,957
By 1920, under a minute.

55
00:03:44,957 --> 00:03:46,869
By 1950, about 7 seconds.

56
00:03:47,519 --> 00:03:49,405
And it wasn't just cheaper.

57
00:03:49,405 --> 00:03:50,313
It was safer.

58
00:03:50,313 --> 00:03:57,157
In 1900, about one in three fires in New York's tenement buildings was caused by lamps or candles.

59
00:03:57,107 --> 00:03:59,364
Light stopped being something you rationed...

60
00:03:59,364 --> 00:04:01,821
and became something you didn't even think about.

61
00:04:04,221 --> 00:04:05,661
Here's where it gets strange.

62
00:04:06,111 --> 00:04:09,023
Official price statistics don't measure light.

63
00:04:09,023 --> 00:04:11,112
They measure the price of things.

64
00:04:11,112 --> 00:04:13,391
Candles, lamp oil, gas, electricity.

65
00:04:15,491 --> 00:04:21,667
But a kerosene lamp gives far more light per drop than a candle, and a bulb far more per dollar than a lamp.

66
00:04:21,667 --> 00:04:26,356
The price of the fuel can go up, while the price of light falls through the floor.

67
00:04:27,006 --> 00:04:36,867
When Nordhaus compared the two, he found that traditional price indexes had overstated the rise in the price of light by a factor of 900 to 1,600.

68
00:04:37,767 --> 00:04:41,432
And if prices are measured wrong, so is wealth.

69
00:04:41,432 --> 00:04:46,892
Official numbers say real wages grew about 13 times from 1800 to 1992.

70
00:04:46,892 --> 00:04:51,025
Nordhaus estimated the true figure might be 40 times.

71
00:04:51,025 --> 00:04:52,195
Maybe far more.

72
00:04:52,245 --> 00:04:55,796
In other words, we may be much richer than the statistics say.

73
00:04:55,796 --> 00:05:01,696
We just stopped noticing, because the things that got cheapest are the things we no longer think about.

74
00:05:05,796 --> 00:05:06,394
Myth check.

75
00:05:06,394 --> 00:05:09,655
Did people really sleep in two shifts before electric light?

76
00:05:09,905 --> 00:05:20,347
The historian Roger Ekirch found references to a first sleep and a second sleep in court records, medical books and letters, in English, French, Italian and Latin.

77
00:05:20,597 --> 00:05:26,873
People went to bed early, woke for an hour or so in the dark, to pray, talk, tend the fire, then slept again.

78
00:05:27,023 --> 00:05:33,577
Ekirch links its decline to cheaper artificial light and changing habits during the Industrial Revolution.

79
00:05:33,827 --> 00:05:37,959
But other researchers point out that it doesn't appear everywhere.

80
00:05:37,959 --> 00:05:41,090
One sleep historian found no trace of it in Japan.

81
00:05:41,090 --> 00:05:44,033
And the evidence is suggestive, not definitive.

82
00:05:44,283 --> 00:05:45,725
Verdict: partly true.

83
00:05:45,725 --> 00:05:50,532
Common in many old records, but how universal it was is still debated.

84
00:05:52,132 --> 00:05:53,688
So, back to that switch.

85
00:05:53,688 --> 00:06:01,341
For the electricity alone, today's thousand lumen-hours costs an average American worker about two-tenths of a second.

86
00:06:01,491 --> 00:06:09,366
That's roughly 100,000 times cheaper than in 1800, and more than 700,000 times cheaper than in Babylon.

87
00:06:09,516 --> 00:06:12,853
Every everyday thing has a price history like this.

88
00:06:12,853 --> 00:06:18,087
Next time: frozen water, and the strange business of shipping ice across oceans.
